1
00:00:01,180 --> 00:00:02,520
The call everyone stopped for was just a squirrel.

2
00:00:02,570 --> 00:00:08,770
It sat on a tree. When it calls, its tail goes up and wags.

3
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,220
On that call alone, about 10 jeeps stood still.

4
00:00:17,270 --> 00:00:18,810
And it brought the leopard out.

5
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:26,740
Listen to the calls. Not only the tiger's. Everyone's.

6
00:00:26,790 --> 00:00:32,990
A call is not always for a tiger. It can be for a leopard.

7
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:40,410
We think a peacock's alarm means a big cat is here.

8
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:45,350
Often it is only a snake or a jungle cat.

9
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:48,640
MOHIT: Which animals usually give alarm calls?

10
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:50,590
Any animal that feels a threat.

11
00:00:50,640 --> 00:00:56,590
Jungle fowl, peafowl, sambar, all the deer.

12
00:00:56,640 --> 00:01:02,840
Barking deer, hog deer, spotted deer.

13
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:09,130
MOHIT: A guest told me langurs and deer call when they see a tiger.

14
00:01:20,150 --> 00:01:26,350
MOHIT: Is it always for a tiger, or for any threat?

15
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:36,940
A jungle fowl need not be calling for a tiger. It can be an eagle.

16
00:01:44,410 --> 00:01:50,610
Or a marten. Corbett has yellow-throated martens.

17
00:01:55,190 --> 00:01:57,840
Wherever they feel a threat, they call.

18
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:00,990
Absolutely. That is how it works.

19
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:07,240
The call means: I have seen you.

20
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:23,220
In Panna, a leopard was in full stalking mode, a deer right beside it.

21
00:02:28,470 --> 00:02:33,760
We thought: first safari, today we get a kill.

22
00:02:33,810 --> 00:02:37,700
But the deer smelled it, thanks to the wind.

23
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:40,320
It called.

24
00:02:40,370 --> 00:02:44,310
The leopard understood: nothing can be done now.

25
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:50,560
It gave up and walked away, right past the deer.

26
00:02:56,770 --> 00:03:02,970
The same happened in Pilibhit: a tiger stalking, a deer ahead.

27
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:07,970
One deer gave the alarm call.

28
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:11,520
The tiger relaxed and let it go.

29
00:03:11,570 --> 00:03:12,070
Often, one call sets off the next.

30
00:03:18,130 --> 00:03:23,900
The whole herd learns: a tiger is near.

31
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:30,150
The tiger knows everyone is alert. It comes back later.

32
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,350
Their message: we are safe now.

33
00:03:36,970 --> 00:03:43,170
Jhalana. We had been looking for a leopard for a long time.

34
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:14,150
On one track, 3 or 4 jeeps were parked. Ours was behind.

35
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:22,600
I asked: whose call is this?

36
00:04:22,650 --> 00:04:28,850
Everyone said: this call has been going for ages. A leopard is here.

37
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:35,120
It was Flora, a leopard, with her cub.

38
00:04:37,230 --> 00:04:42,610
Would she walk the cub across, or carry it in her mouth?

39
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:47,200
We all stood up.

40
00:04:47,250 --> 00:04:50,150
The driver said: come here. I climbed onto his seat.

41
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:51,900
And Flora walked out.

42
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:55,940
Cub in her mouth, she crossed the track and was gone.

43
00:04:55,990 --> 00:05:00,630
The next day the photo was in the paper. (his memory)

44
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:06,880
The call everyone stopped for was a squirrel.

45
00:05:21,710 --> 00:05:25,780
We saw the leopard, and got a good photo.

46
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:31,260
Leopards have names too, in some areas.

47
00:05:31,310 --> 00:05:37,510
Where tigers dominate, like Panna, leopards usually have no names. They are seen rarely.

48
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:45,410
In leopard areas like Jhalana and Rajaji, every leopard has a name.

49
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:50,390
Jhalana: Rana, Bagheera, Flora.

50
00:05:50,440 --> 00:05:56,640
Rajaji: Heera, Panna, Moti, Diana, Laila, Leela, Mala.

51
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:13,940
Jeep guides and forest staff give the names, to identify them.

52
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:21,280
How do you tell leopards apart? By their patterns.

53
00:06:25,110 --> 00:06:31,310
Leopard is also called Guldar, Bagheera, Tendua.

54
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:48,600
Every leopard has different spots, and a different colour.

55
00:06:48,650 --> 00:06:54,850
The leopard Panna has a magenta tone. Heera is brighter.

56
00:07:02,240 --> 00:07:02,910
Eye colour and eye shape differ too.

57
00:07:04,530 --> 00:07:10,730
Do not push your guide and driver: "show me a tiger".

58
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:17,240
Go 10 times: 9 times the tiger has already seen you. (his rule of thumb)

59
00:07:24,650 --> 00:07:30,850
Not every tiger is bold. Some are shy.

60
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:59,700
The more your jeep moves, the less a shy tiger comes out.

61
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:06,400
Park 30 to 90 minutes where you see pug marks.

62
00:08:22,620 --> 00:08:28,820
Watch the activity around: deer, birds, other jeeps.

63
00:08:31,240 --> 00:08:37,440
Ask other jeeps. They share where an alarm call came from.

64
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:42,800
A sambar's alarm call is considered the strongest.

65
00:08:42,850 --> 00:08:48,510
Weak eyesight, very sharp nose. (Hitesh's field experience)

66
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:54,760
It calls for the predator that threatens it, not small things.

67
00:08:56,900 --> 00:08:58,770
Do not rely on a deer's call so much.

68
00:08:58,820 --> 00:09:01,620
On a sambar's call, stop right there.

69
00:09:01,670 --> 00:09:03,160
Sambar calls for a leopard or a tiger.

